WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.260
Now, we know you're a pretty good liar,

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:04.270
but what if you're caught by the enemy

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.610
and faced with a lie-detector test?

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:10.451
Michele Rigby Assad: OK, if
you are being polygraphed,

00:00:10.451 --> 00:00:12.259
it's already too late.

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:14.320
Hi, I'm Michele Rigby Assad,

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.160
a former intelligence
officer with the CIA.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.590
I spent the majority of my career

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.830
in the Middle East doing counterterrorism

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.500
and counterintelligence work.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.130
And today we're going to review television

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.413
and movie clips to see,

00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:29.271
are they real or not?

00:00:33.693 --> 00:00:34.710
Sydney: Do you see anything?

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.530
Dixon: Get closer, I can't tell
if he's got the card on him.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.680
Sydney: Anything?

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.000
Dixon: Yeah, the key card's
in his breast pocket.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.666
Michele: The idea of having an earpiece

00:00:43.666 --> 00:00:47.083
where you have immediate support

00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:48.290
and targeting assistance

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:51.333
as you're carrying out your operation,

00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:53.980
no, that does not happen.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.110
I have never used multiple
wigs during an operation

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.960
or different sets of clothes.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.070
I have changed my appearance.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:06.365
I have put my hair up into
a ponytail or put a hat on

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:08.970
or changed a shirt or taken a shirt off.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.761
It's very rare in your career

00:01:10.761 --> 00:01:15.211
to require a big change of appearance

00:01:15.211 --> 00:01:17.794
like you would see here in this clip.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.790
Dixon: Hold that for 30 seconds.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.480
The duplicate key card's printing.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.608
Michele: The high-tech
nature of that piece,

00:01:25.608 --> 00:01:28.160
being able to read the key card

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.301
and be able to then create
a new one based off of that,

00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:34.360
I've never heard of that before.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.233
Man: Wonderful. Yes, yes, well, of course,

00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:38.300
getting funding is very difficult,

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.350
particularly in today's...

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:40.410
Michele: It's the oddest thing.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.540
You can be on the edge of the earth

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.330
in a place that's not very well known,

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.955
and you could actually recognize

00:01:46.955 --> 00:01:48.646
a CIA classmate or colleague,

00:01:48.646 --> 00:01:50.850
but you do not cross paths.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.100
You do not acknowledge each other,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.641
because you could both be in
the middle of an operation.

00:01:55.641 --> 00:01:59.402
And we try really hard to
de-conflict operations,

00:01:59.402 --> 00:02:01.740
so you don't want to greet
them with the wrong name.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.262
So you just kind of, like,

00:02:03.262 --> 00:02:05.450
wink at each other and keep walking.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.325
So, I would rate this clip as a one.

00:02:09.325 --> 00:02:11.016
Not realistic at all.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.633
Kittridge: I know how
much Jim, in particular,

00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:18.620
meant to you, Ethan.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:19.700
Ethan: Yeah.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.227
Michele: It isn't unusual to have

00:02:21.227 --> 00:02:23.620
an operational meeting in a restaurant.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.290
They are seated right next

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.670
to the glass front of the building.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.460
You would never sit there,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.760
because presumably anybody walking by

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.640
on the street has a clear view

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.540
of this operational meeting taking place.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.723
With high-end restaurants,

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:40.331
you're going to get
waiters and waitstaff there

00:02:40.331 --> 00:02:43.683
incredibly attentive to
your needs as a diner,

00:02:43.683 --> 00:02:45.500
and you don't want that.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.008
You want to scope out the restaurant

00:02:48.008 --> 00:02:50.541
and get a seat towards maybe the back

00:02:50.541 --> 00:02:52.330
or the side of a restaurant.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.190
You don't want it too noisy.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.980
You don't want it too quiet.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.310
And you also want to know
where the back entrance is.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.251
So you want to be able to come and go

00:03:02.251 --> 00:03:04.884
from separate entrances and exits

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.250
after meeting with a clandestine source.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.024
So, this whole scenario
would have never actually

00:03:10.024 --> 00:03:12.130
taken place in real life.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.020
Ethan: Why was there another team?

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:14.853
Kittridge: What?

00:03:14.853 --> 00:03:17.920
Ethan: Of IMF agents
at the embassy tonight.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.340
Michele: Absolutely not.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.350
That would never, ever happen,

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.833
and the reason is,

00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:25.124
you have to have a full understanding

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:27.720
of everyone involved in the operation.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.063
Everything you've done
is fully planned out

00:03:30.063 --> 00:03:32.590
and de-conflicted with people in the area.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.080
So if you were to notice the other team,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.244
you would have called out the
operation from the beginning.

00:03:38.244 --> 00:03:40.440
Kittridge: I can understand
you're very upset.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.960
Ethan: Kittridge, you've
never seen me very upset.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.140
Michele: That part is accurate.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.027
The sense of no matter what happens,

00:03:46.027 --> 00:03:48.810
you have to keep your head on straight.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.985
You have to focus and
think, OK, what next?

00:03:54.811 --> 00:03:56.017
[explosion]

00:03:57.125 --> 00:03:59.050
They certainly make intel officers

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.120
look incredibly cool,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.737
and who doesn't want explosive gum?

00:04:03.737 --> 00:04:04.745
Love that.

00:04:04.745 --> 00:04:06.620
I would give it

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.197
a two out of 10 for realism,

00:04:09.197 --> 00:04:11.970
which doesn't mean I don't
absolutely love this movie.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.290
I do, but not realistic at all.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.760
Patrick: I was given the
specific instructions by Elaine

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.483
to tailor these gadgets to you.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.780
Michele: Nothing on that table,

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.810
we wouldn't be using
anything of that nature.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.238
The things that we are assigned

00:04:27.238 --> 00:04:28.596
when we go to a war zone

00:04:28.596 --> 00:04:31.046
are things like your Glock or your helmet

00:04:31.046 --> 00:04:32.500
or your Kevlar vest.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.766
When you are having an operation

00:04:34.766 --> 00:04:36.999
that requires a piece of technology,

00:04:36.999 --> 00:04:40.000
then you will go through
a very long process

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.970
of figuring out what the best
tech is for that scenario.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.960
It's not issued to you
until you request it,

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.510
until you require that.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.583
Patrick: This antifungal spray can freeze

00:04:50.583 --> 00:04:53.200
and disable any security system.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.308
Michele: What I love about

00:04:54.308 --> 00:04:56.191
the silly items that he gives her,

00:04:56.191 --> 00:04:59.760
are things she can stash in
her purse and her suitcase

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.930
and no one's ever really
going to think twice about.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.740
The idea is to not stand out.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.190
She's successful in this movie

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.800
because she's the unexpected spy.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.160
You don't want people to take note of you.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.230
We talk about lulling people to sleep.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.260
Backstopping, like
you're creating a persona

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.501
and the persona's that you're just

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:22.051
a normal, everyday person,

00:05:22.051 --> 00:05:25.220
and you even need things like this.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.045
I give it a three.

00:05:28.203 --> 00:05:31.778
But of course for fun I give it a 10.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.030
Bourne: I can tell you
the license-plate numbers

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.855
of all six cars outside.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.820
I can tell you that our
waitress is left-handed

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.442
and the guy sitting up at
the counter weighs 215 pounds

00:05:45.442 --> 00:05:47.470
and knows how to handle himself.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.340
Michele: Now, his powers of
recall are quite incredible

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.180
and unrealistic.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.930
Essentially, you're trained
to remember people's profiles

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.515
when they stand out,
when they do something

00:06:00.515 --> 00:06:02.700
that's unusual or different.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.320
You're conducting a
surveillance detection route.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.990
So you're making sure
nobody's going to follow you

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.593
to your clandestine meeting.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.776
You want to make sure that you don't

00:06:11.776 --> 00:06:14.051
see the same cars twice,

00:06:14.051 --> 00:06:16.562
and so one technique that they teach us

00:06:16.562 --> 00:06:18.870
is to speak out loud

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.530
a description of the various
vehicles that you're seeing.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.989
So, it's called commentary driving.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.437
This whole scenario

00:06:29.437 --> 00:06:32.620
would have never actually
taken place in real life.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.930
We learn how to use our car as a weapon

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.140
in case we're ambushed.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.520
We learn how to drive a car.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.930
Really drive it to its limits.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:45.020
How to avoid impacting
casuals in the field.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.250
I mean, it's the best.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:48.690
It's, like, the coolest
part of our training,

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.260
but you hope you will never use any of it.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.850
And so I was driving in my car alone,

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:57.880
and I was in the process of
possibly getting carjacked.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:02.745
I had to use my vehicle to hit someone

00:07:02.745 --> 00:07:04.862
a couple of times to get them to move

00:07:04.862 --> 00:07:07.880
so I could drive out of harm's way,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.010
and I didn't think too
much what I was doing about

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.840
as I was in the middle of this scenario,

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.230
but my training kicked in, thank God,

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:18.867
and I was able to get away,

00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:22.530
and thankfully I also
didn't hurt that person.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:27.030
I would give it a two
out of 10 for realism.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:36.338
The idea that we shoot people

00:07:36.338 --> 00:07:39.500
or we have black belts in martial arts

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.710
and we take people down with a kick,

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.900
the psychology of espionage
is very on the down low

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.170
and not in-your-face
action-packed sequences.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.260
We did get some physical training,

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.080
and most of it was, you know,

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.710
what you do if things go really bad.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.600
We get qualified on a Glock,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.404
and we feel quite comfortable with a Glock

00:08:00.404 --> 00:08:01.600
after that training.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:04.683
So I would rate this clip as a one.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:08.650
Jack: The game. When we were in Paris,

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:11.030
we uncovered correspondence
between Suleiman

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:12.300
and his brother, Ali.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.638
Michele: So, the way the scene is set up,

00:08:14.638 --> 00:08:17.680
with a room full of intelligence officers

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.070
from very, very senior
level all the way down

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:24.090
to junior-type officers, is quite unusual.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.673
It might remind you

00:08:25.673 --> 00:08:27.323
of something you have seen in the press,

00:08:27.323 --> 00:08:29.560
and that is President Obama

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.560
sitting with his closest security
and intelligence advisors

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:37.890
as the operation against
Osama bin Laden unraveled.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.250
They were right there along the way,

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:40.770
they heard the communications,

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.570
and you see them sitting
in the room together.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.440
So, most of the time that doesn't happen.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.256
That would have to be an incredibly

00:08:46.256 --> 00:08:48.114
high-level-type operation,

00:08:48.114 --> 00:08:51.207
but you would always
have people in the room

00:08:51.207 --> 00:08:53.470
who are subject-matter experts.

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:55.940
You could speak up as a junior officer,

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:57.790
because you may be the
only one in the room

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.590
who actually has the substance

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.780
or the knowledge about your target.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.763
Jack: They were using
a video-game system's

00:09:04.763 --> 00:09:07.472
online-messaging app to communicate.

00:09:07.472 --> 00:09:10.387
Michele: So, terrorists
and other criminals

00:09:10.387 --> 00:09:14.738
have actually used the
chat function in games

00:09:14.738 --> 00:09:16.060
to communicate with each other.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.870
They have used it as a form
of covert communications.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:24.670
We have the calculation
of risk versus gain.

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:28.040
So, would we try to insert
ourselves in a relationship

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.690
between two really close brothers?

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:34.830
You would walk into that
probably understanding

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.877
that you would not be able to have

00:09:36.877 --> 00:09:40.080
the proper verbal paroles
to make the connection.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.290
Jack: Who's got this?

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.520
Agent: Let's go, guys!
Get on it, get on it.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.428
Michele: Most of our operations are not as

00:09:45.428 --> 00:09:48.040
quickly unfolding or as rapid as this one.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:50.250
The CIA is very much a bureaucracy,

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.390
like every US government agency,

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:55.200
and it takes us a long time

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.690
to plan, implement, execute operations.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:01.957
There are very, very few
instances in your career

00:10:01.957 --> 00:10:03.967
where something like that would happen,

00:10:03.967 --> 00:10:05.639
where you're kind of jumping through hoops

00:10:05.639 --> 00:10:07.850
and looking up information quickly.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.450
I mean, we do do that, but it's not,

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:14.463
it's not as prevalent as this
clip would make you think.

00:10:17.008 --> 00:10:18.508
Agent: He's still online, Jack.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:25.880
Michele: I love the authenticity

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:27.910
of showing how a terrorist operates,

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.908
especially using the gaming chat.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.468
Although, you know,
Hollywood amps it up a bit,

00:10:33.468 --> 00:10:36.168
but because of all that,
I'd give it about a seven.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:49.751
This clip makes me sweat,

00:10:49.751 --> 00:10:52.260
because it is so realistic

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:56.010
in terms of what we deal with
when we're crossing borders

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.670
or getting ready to board a flight

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.545
or enter or exit a country.

00:10:59.545 --> 00:11:02.094
It can be incredibly dicey.

00:11:02.094 --> 00:11:04.950
Like, I feel all sweaty,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:08.240
and my heart is racing even
thinking about it right now.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.938
You have to be so confident

00:11:11.938 --> 00:11:15.400
in keeping your cover and
maintaining your legend.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:18.992
Even if your insides feel like
they're literally exploding,

00:11:18.992 --> 00:11:20.910
you cannot show that on your face.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:23.340
You have to remember who
you are, what your name is,

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.030
what you're doing, what your
purpose was in the country,

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.880
when you entered, when you exited.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:30.380
In terms of your cover story,

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:33.230
you want to keep it as close
to the truth as possible

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:35.000
because you have to memorize it

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:36.830
and you have to recall it easily

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:39.222
and you have to recall it under stress.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:48.660
This movie is based on
a real-life situation,

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:52.630
which is right after the
1979 Iranian Revolution.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.170
Some Americans were able
to escape the embassy

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.585
and with the help of
the Canadian government

00:11:57.585 --> 00:11:59.360
come up with this
operation to get them out.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:03.250
So not only is it realistic,
but it really did happen.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:05.900
They did actually have university students

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:08.190
putting those photos together

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.710
to try to figure out the identities

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:14.040
of all of the US embassy staff.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:15.790
Guard: What was the purpose of your visit?

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.140
Bob: We are looking for
locations for a film.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.090
Michele: Airport
authorities don't even have

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.550
to have a good reason to detain you

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.450
or to stop you from boarding a flight.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:27.410
If they don't like the way you look

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:28.850
or they don't trust you

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:30.650
or you say something wrong,

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.420
that could change the
course of your entire life.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:37.240
Technology has changed
a great deal since 1979,

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.538
when this happened, and it's
making it more difficult

00:12:40.538 --> 00:12:43.472
for intelligence officers and others

00:12:43.472 --> 00:12:45.360
to cross borders covertly,

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.150
because now we've got
all kinds of thumbprints

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:52.300
and eye scanners and
all kinds of equipment

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.850
that's used to establish your identity.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:56.525
This one I give a nine,

00:12:56.525 --> 00:13:00.920
because it's so close to accurate.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.640
Lynne: I really don't
want to do this anymore,

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:03.740
and I mean it, Carrie.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.020
I feel like I can't even breathe now.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:07.062
Carrie: All you have to do now

00:13:07.062 --> 00:13:08.536
is keep your eyes and ears open

00:13:08.536 --> 00:13:10.840
for the remainder of your
contract with the prince.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:12.506
Michele: So, I love this clip,

00:13:12.506 --> 00:13:15.206
because it really shows
the bread and butter

00:13:15.206 --> 00:13:17.420
of what it is to be an
intelligence officer.

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:21.330
The way that she handles
her source is not ideal.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:24.320
She basically said, "You have to do this."

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:25.590
Oh, my goodness!

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.920
You would never say that to a real source.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:29.750
They are there because
they want to be there.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:32.250
They are there because
they understand the risk

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:34.230
and they're willing to take the risk.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:37.630
Now, what you have to do
is, if they're nervous,

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.670
if they're concerned about moving forward

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:41.910
or they feel like their life is in danger,

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:43.950
you have to talk them through that.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:46.541
You have to train them.

00:13:46.541 --> 00:13:49.366
And so the idea of telling her, like,

00:13:49.366 --> 00:13:50.993
"You have to do this,"

00:13:50.993 --> 00:13:53.720
is the worst way to treat a source.

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:55.970
Every time you have a source meeting,

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.260
if there's a lot on the
line with the source,

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:01.780
it's almost like you have to
re-recruit them every time.

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:03.708
Carrie: You are the only agency asset

00:14:03.708 --> 00:14:05.810
to have had eyes on Nazir in seven years.

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:06.680
Lynne: Asset?

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.280
Michele: We do in fact
call our sources assets,

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.740
but we would never do that to their face,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:14.550
because it's a very inhuman
way to refer to them,

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.130
and they are human beings.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.414
Lynne: I am just some
girl from Sandusky, Ohio,

00:14:19.414 --> 00:14:21.117
you talked into helping out her country.

00:14:21.117 --> 00:14:23.150
Carrie: And I won't let
anything happen to you.

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:26.500
Michele: We are not God that
we can protect our sources.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.250
There are certain things
in certain situations

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:32.060
that we can do to help our sources out,

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:33.300
but at the end of the day,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.770
they're the ones on the front lines.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:38.550
They're the ones in difficult situations,

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.030
and they're the ones who
have to make a decision.

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:45.363
So you have to help them
think through scenarios,

00:14:45.363 --> 00:14:48.360
ways to ensure that they
keep themselves safe

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.350
and don't take any unnecessary risks.

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:54.750
Your sources may not intuitively
know what's too risky,

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:56.040
what they can get away with,

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:57.000
and if, at the end of the day,

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.541
they don't feel comfortable,

00:14:58.541 --> 00:15:00.424
they can walk away at any time.

00:15:00.424 --> 00:15:04.363
But the part of this
that is inaccurate to me

00:15:04.363 --> 00:15:07.270
is the fact that a Western woman

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:10.022
who is the girlfriend of a sheikh

00:15:10.022 --> 00:15:15.022
would be anywhere remotely
close to a wanted terrorist.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:17.010
That would not happen.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.760
She is unvetted, untested.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.050
Fundamentally, she's a Western woman.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:26.370
So that whole scenario
is extremely unlikely.

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:29.153
I would give this clip an eight,

00:15:29.153 --> 00:15:31.630
only because it is the closest thing

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:34.730
to a real-life covert
meeting with a source

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:38.118
that I have ever seen in
the movies or on television.

00:15:38.118 --> 00:15:39.800
JJ: Now, we know you're
a pretty good liar,

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:42.016
but what if you're caught by the enemy

00:15:42.016 --> 00:15:44.250
and faced with a lie-detector test?

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:48.200
Michele: OK, if you are being polygraphed,

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:50.091
it's already too late.

00:15:50.091 --> 00:15:54.110
If you are arrested or detained
by a foreign government

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:56.460
and you're being polygraphed,

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.100
done. You're finished.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:00.414
You don't even want to get to that point.

00:16:00.414 --> 00:16:02.383
JJ: The key is to focus on your breathing.

00:16:02.383 --> 00:16:04.390
The slightest increase
in your blood pressure

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:06.620
is an indicator that you're
not telling the truth.

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.503
Michele: So, no, we are not trained

00:16:08.503 --> 00:16:11.220
how to defeat the polygraph,

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.540
because, you know, once that happens,

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.700
you're already probably imprisoned.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:19.390
So that's not accurate at all.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:22.062
The agency uses the polygraph

00:16:22.062 --> 00:16:25.090
as part of your security and
background investigation.

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:28.364
So basically if you train
someone to defeat it,

00:16:28.364 --> 00:16:30.817
you're training your employees to defeat

00:16:30.817 --> 00:16:34.810
one of the methods you
use to keep them honest.

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:38.003
So they would never want
to do something like that.

00:16:38.003 --> 00:16:39.160
Nathan: I can probably make arrangements

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.560
for you to end your tour
of duty in San Diego,

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.190
in which case I'll presume you'll forget

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:44.660
we ever had this conversation.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:48.100
Michele: Getting personally
recruited in that manner,

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:50.760
so, while I know a couple of people

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:51.800
that that happened to,

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:55.282
the vast majority of us
actually apply to the CIA

00:16:55.282 --> 00:16:58.270
online or through someone that we know.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:00.410
Nathan: The next day, we
started his tradecraft.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:01.418
Michele: Wow.

00:17:01.418 --> 00:17:05.780
No, it could take an entire
year for the CIA to vet you,

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:07.830
and you have to go through

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:11.746
so many processes of polygraphs

00:17:11.746 --> 00:17:15.900
and very long interviews and psych exams,

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.280
and so you have to go
through a battery of tests

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:19.930
before you get to the part

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:21.630
where you're actually
going through training.

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:23.800
Now, in terms of your tradecraft training,

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:25.900
that takes about a year,

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:28.740
and it's very, very intense,

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.970
and it's not one-on-one like you see here.

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:33.570
You have classroom time,

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:36.330
and then you have role
playing in the field,

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:39.990
and you do go out in public
and you practice your skills.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:42.063
So that part is accurate.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:44.938
Nathan: Always carry
cigarettes and a lighter, too.

00:17:44.938 --> 00:17:45.838
Great icebreaker.

00:17:45.838 --> 00:17:48.120
Michele: Now, the whole
"cigarettes and a lighter,"

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:49.437
they can be very useful,

00:17:49.437 --> 00:17:50.786
especially in parts of the world

00:17:50.786 --> 00:17:52.494
where smoking is a big thing.

00:17:52.494 --> 00:17:54.810
So, I spent a lot of
time in the Middle East,

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:56.620
and a lot of people smoked.

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.153
They were quite useful for

00:17:58.153 --> 00:18:01.045
when you were serving in
really dangerous places,

00:18:01.045 --> 00:18:03.027
where there are a lot of military

00:18:03.027 --> 00:18:04.344
or criminal checkpoints.

00:18:04.344 --> 00:18:06.737
A pack of cigarettes
or a box of cigarettes

00:18:06.737 --> 00:18:08.690
could potentially save your life.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:10.740
It's very valuable in
certain parts of the world,

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.690
so you would keep that in
your glove-compartment box.

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:15.773
Nathan: The suit in the kitchen.

00:18:16.756 --> 00:18:18.120
Threat?

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:19.470
Tom: Wait, how'd you see that?

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:25.260
Michele: Every time we can use a mirror

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:29.707
or a glass door or a glass
window to see a reflection,

00:18:29.707 --> 00:18:31.010
oh, that's the best.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:34.040
Intelligence officers love reflections.

00:18:34.040 --> 00:18:35.990
Nathan: Solicit information from someone.

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:38.325
Tom: Gladly.

00:18:38.325 --> 00:18:40.452
Michele: Elicitation is one
of the most important skills

00:18:40.452 --> 00:18:41.960
that you have as an intelligence officer.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:42.870
So, you would need to be able

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:45.790
to talk people into doing things for you.

00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:48.490
You need to have the
emotional intelligence

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:50.854
that allows you to make a connection

00:18:50.854 --> 00:18:54.368
to ultimately recruit and handle sources,

00:18:54.368 --> 00:18:56.859
or just to kind of get through your day,

00:18:56.859 --> 00:18:59.485
maintain your cover and your, you know,

00:18:59.485 --> 00:19:02.085
your lifestyle abroad, whatever that is.

00:19:02.085 --> 00:19:04.390
But those are really critical skills.

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:05.990
I rate this a seven.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:06.981
It's...

00:19:06.981 --> 00:19:09.100
there's some inauthentic pieces,

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.570
but I like it as a whole.

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:13.061
Roger: Mr. Al-Masri?

00:19:14.195 --> 00:19:15.737
Mr. Omar Sadiki.

00:19:15.737 --> 00:19:16.795
Sadiki: As-salamu alaikum.

00:19:16.795 --> 00:19:18.380
Al-Masri: Wa alaikum assalaam.

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:19.810
[camera clicking]

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:23.515
Michele: So, what I find
inauthentic about this scene

00:19:23.515 --> 00:19:26.410
is that you have your target

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:30.000
and then an operator
almost crossing paths.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.770
That would never happen in real life.

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.750
So, of course, what's happening here

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:35.300
is the target is being set up

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:37.920
to be photographed with
another individual.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:39.580
You would have coordinated,

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:41.540
in an operation like
this, way ahead of time,

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:45.070
and not in the actual office
where it's going to occur.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.452
This is what you call
the operational bubble,

00:19:47.452 --> 00:19:50.370
and you have to get outside
that operational bubble

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.870
to do something like photograph these two.

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:55.580
And you saw this huge
lens that he's using.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.740
That's going to grab a lot of attention.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:00.710
So it's highly likely
someone would have seen him

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:03.170
in the car with this ginormous lens.

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:04.486
If there'd been another car

00:20:04.486 --> 00:20:07.044
on the other side of him, possibly,

00:20:07.044 --> 00:20:09.020
but he wouldn't even need a lens that big,

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:10.960
because he's really not that far away.

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:14.340
We would have definitely
created a scenario

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.270
where you get your target
into a specific location,

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:18.911
but you want to protect

00:20:18.911 --> 00:20:22.040
your technical asset who's
taking the photograph.

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:25.360
So you want them to be outside
the operational bubble.

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:28.360
So that's the part that's
not authentic here.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.801
I'd probably give it around a six,

00:20:30.801 --> 00:20:33.160
so slightly authentic,

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:35.210
but there are a lot of problems here,

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:36.668
as we've pointed out.

00:20:36.668 --> 00:20:39.890
Who doesn't love Melissa
McCarthy in "Spy"?

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.170
I laugh out loud at this movie.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:43.823
She is so brilliant.

